{
  "abstract": "Explicit one-tape, two-symbol Turing machines with a designated beep state (Aaronson's beeping busy beaver convention), given as literal transition tables, together with core-Lean proofs (no Mathlib) that each machine, run from the blank tape, beeps only finitely often exactly when a stated Sigma_2 sentence holds. The flagship machine (278 states) is equivalent to the Sigma_2 form of P = NP: some candidate program decides a fixed self-padded NP-complete language within a polynomial clock on every instance. The equivalence of that sentence with P = NP is also proved in Lean (ZP.Cx.sentence_iff_PeqNP) for the Turing-machine models defined in the development. The same guest interpreter yields machines for further Sigma_2 statements: P = PP and P = PSPACE, factoring, discrete logarithm, graph isomorphism, an NTT-based variant over least primes 1 + n i, linear-time integer multiplication on multitape machines, a co-NP verifier variant, promise-P = promise-BPP, NP in P/poly, deterministic and nondeterministic multihead log-space (L = NL), and linear-time multiplication of matrices over GF(2) on multitape machines. A 19-state machine quasihalts iff there are finitely many twin primes.",
